# Line endings for the two start scripts, pinned rather than left to whatever
# core.autocrlf each contributor happens to have set.
#
# This is not tidiness.  A shell script checked out with CRLF endings has a
# carriage return stuck on the end of its shebang, so the kernel looks for an
# interpreter called "/bin/sh\r", does not find one, and reports the confusing
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".  On a Windows clone with
# autocrlf=true that is exactly what would happen to start-pokepack.sh.
# Everything is LF in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# Not a style preference -- src/luawrite.js writes options.lua with \n, and a
# test asserts the writer is byte-compatible with the engine by re-encoding a
# fixture and comparing it to itself. Check that fixture out with CRLF and the
# comparison fails on line endings nobody typed, which is exactly what happened
# on the Windows runner: it clones with autocrlf on, so a file that is LF in the
# repo and LF everywhere the code looks arrived as CRLF and failed a byte test.
#
# text=auto still lets git detect and skip real binaries.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe is happiest with CRLF, and can
# mis-read labels and goto targets without it.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
